  "raw_text": "Below is a 12‑week, pregnancy‑safe strength program designed for an advanced lifter who is 20 weeks pregnant now and has OB clearance for moderate strength training. It keeps you strong, supports posture and pelvic floor, avoids supine/maximum efforts, and adapts as the pregnancy progresses. Use this as a template and adjust loads, exercise selection and volume to how she feels each day.\n\nImportant rules to follow every session\n- No supine (lying on back) or prone (lying on stomach) exercises.  \n- No maximal lifts or 1RM testing; keep loads submaximal (RPE 6–8 / 10). Avoid Valsalva — cue steady diaphragmatic breathing and “brace with an open glottis.”  \n- Avoid contact sports, sudden direction changes, breath‑holding, and anything that causes abdominal bulging or coning.  \n- Monitor: stop and contact OB if she has bleeding, leakage, dizziness, chest pain, regular painful contractions, reduced fetal movement, or any new severe pain.  \n- Hydration, temperature control (avoid overheating), and rest between sets are essential.  \n- Frequent pelvic floor cueing (Kegels) and gentle core activation are included. Progressions/modifications provided.\n\nSession frequency and structure\n- Strength: 3 sessions per week (A / B / C). Aim 48+ hours between heavy sessions if possible.  \n- Optional: 1 light conditioning + mobility session per week (walking, swimming, stationary bike, light elliptical) — low impact, RPE <6.  \n- Warm‑up each session: 5–10 min low‑impact cardio + dynamic mobility + 2 warm sets of first compound lift. Finish with breathing/pelvic floor work and mobility.\n\nOverall progression plan (conservative)\n- Weeks 1–4 (Gestational wk ~20–23): Maintain strength. Moderate intensity, slightly reduced volume relative to pre‑pregnancy. Primary lifts 3–4 sets, 6–8 reps.  \n- Weeks 5–8 (wk ~24–27): Transition to more supported/unilateral work and stability. Primary sets 3–4, 6–10 reps. Slightly lower peak loads as balance shifts.  \n- Weeks 9–12 (wk ~28–32): Prioritize safety, posture, pelvic floor; lower loads ~10–20% from week 1 if needed, higher-rep accessory work 10–15 reps for endurance, maintain neural stimulus with moderate sets and controlled tempo.\n\nLoad guidance\n- RPE 6–8 (moderate to moderately hard). No sets taken to absolute failure.  \n- If pre‑pregnancy squat/press loads are used, reduce by ~5–10% on weeks 1–4 if needed and follow the above progressive reductions later as pregnancy advances.  \n- Use machine or seated alternatives for chest press/shoulder press when more comfort or support needed. Use belt or lighter loads if bracing difficult.\n\nCore & pelvic floor (short daily routine)\n- Diaphragmatic breathing with pelvic floor cue (supine contraindicated; do seated, semi‑reclined or quadruped): 3–5 slow breaths, inhale to expand ribs/diaphragm, exhale and gently draw pelvic floor up (submaximal)—5–10 reps.  \n- Pallof press (anti‑rotation) standing: 2–3 x 8–12 s hold (each side) as part of sessions.  \n- Quadruped rocking + bird‑dog is safe (quadruped): 2–3 x 8–10 reps. Avoid intense loaded trunk flexion (no sit‑ups/crunches).\n\nSession templates (no supine)\nTempo notation: e.g., 3‑0‑1 = 3s eccentric, 0s pause, 1s concentric. Rest indicated.\n\nWeeks 1–4: Strength Maintenance (3 x week)\n- Goal: maintain neural strength and muscle mass; controlled compound lifts. Primary sets 3–4, reps 6–8.\n\nSession A — Lower emphasis\n1) Warmup: 5–8 min bike + dynamic hips/ankles + pelvic floor breath work  \n2) Trap‑bar deadlift (or hex bar) — 4 x 6–8 @ RPE 7 (3‑0‑1). Rest 90–120s  \n   Alternative: Romanian deadlift with dumbbells if trap bar uncomfortable.  \n3) Goblet squat or safety‑bar squat (or light barbell back squat with wider stance) — 3 x 6–8. Rest 90s  \n   Cue: wider stance if belly inhibits depth; use box/bench to touch rather than full depth if needed.  \n4) Split‑stance RDL (dumbbells) — 3 x 8 each leg. Rest 60–90s  \n5) Seated or standing cable hip extension / banded hip hinge (for glute emphasis) — 3 x 10–12  \n6) Standing calf raise (single or double) — 3 x 12–15  \n7) Core: Quadruped bird‑dog — 2 x 8–10 each side; Pallof press 2 x 10 each side\n\nSession B — Upper emphasis\n1) Warmup: 5–8 min row/armbike + shoulder mobility + pelvic floor breath work  \n2) Seated/Incline chest press (machine or dumbbells on incline bench >45°) — 4 x 6–8 @ RPE 7. Rest 90s  \n3) Single‑arm cable or dumbbell row (supported chest on incline bench or kneeling) — 3 x 8–10 each arm. Rest 60–90s  \n4) Seated dumbbell overhead press (back supported mini‑incline/bench) or machine press — 3 x 6–8  \n5) Lat pulldown or assisted pull‑up (neutral grip) — 3 x 8–10  \n6) Band face pulls — 3 x 12–15  \n7) Arms: hammer curls 2 x 10; triceps rope pressdown 2 x 10  \n8) Core: Standing Pallof press 2 x 8–10 each side; pelvic floor cueing between sets\n\nSession C — Full body/stability\n1) Warmup + pelvic floor breath work  \n2) Trap‑bar or kettlebell deadlift variant — 3 x 6–8 @ RPE 6–7  \n3) Supported rear foot elevated split squat (TRX or hands on rack) — 3 x 8 each leg  \n4) Seated cable row (or chest‑supported row) — 3 x 8–10  \n5) Single‑arm DB overhead carry or farmer carry (suitcase/DB) — 3 x 40–60s carries (light–moderate)  \n6) Banded clams / standing banded lateral walk — 3 x 12–15  \n7) Core/mobility: Quadruped pelvic tilts + bird‑dog 2 x 8–10; side plank from knees or standing side support 2 x 20–30s each side\n\nOptional conditioning (1x week)\n- 20–35 min low‑impact: walking incline treadmill, stationary bike, swimming, elliptical. Keep RPE <6–7. Avoid overheating; hydrate.\n\nWeeks 5–8: Transition & Stability Emphasis (3 x week)\n- Slightly reduce max loads if balance is affected. Increase unilateral work, mobility and scapular stability. Primary sets 3–4, reps 6–10.\n\nSession A — Lower (adjustments)\n1) Warmup + pelvic floor breath work  \n2) Trap‑bar deadlift or Romanian DL (moderate) — 3 x 6–8  \n3) Bulgarian split squat (assisted TRX or back foot elevated) — 3 x 8–10 each leg  \n4) Cable step‑ups or box step‑ups (hold rail) — 3 x 8–10 each leg  \n5) Standing cable hip extension or quadruped fire hydrant/donkey kicks — 3 x 12–15  \n6) Core: Quadruped bird‑dog 3 x 8; Pallof press 3 x 8–10 each side\n\nSession B — Upper (adjustments)\n1) Warmup  \n2) Seated incline chest press (machine/dumbbell) — 4 x 6–8  \n3) Chest‑supported T‑bar or single‑arm row — 3 x 8–10  \n4) Seated DB shoulder press (moderate) — 3 x 8–10  \n5) Lat pulldown / neutral grip rows — 3 x 8–10  \n6) Band pull‑aparts / face pulls — 3 x 15  \n7) Core: Standing Pallof hold 3 x 10–15s; diaphragmatic breathing sets\n\nSession C — Full body (stability + carry)\n1) Warmup  \n2) Kettlebell single‑leg RDL (supported) — 3 x 8 each leg  \n3) TRX squat to row (or TRX supported squat) — 3 x 10  \n4) Seated machine hip ab/adduction or banded clams — 3 x 12–15  \n5) Farmer carries / overhead carry (light) — 3 x 30–60s  \n6) Core: Side plank from knees 2 x 20–30s each side; quadruped cat/cow mobility\n\nWeeks 9–12: Maintenance, Posture & Endurance (3 x week)\n- As belly increases, reduce heavy bilateral loading, further prioritize safe balance and mobility. Reps shift higher for accessory work (10–15). Primary lifts 3 sets x 6–10 at conservative RPE 6–7.\n\nSession A — Lower (conservative)\n1) Warmup  \n2) Trap‑bar deadlift (lighter/moderate) or dumbbell RDL — 3 x 6–8 @ RPE 6–7  \n3) Goblet or box squat (wider stance, higher box) — 3 x 8–10  \n4) Supported single‑leg squat or step‑ups holding rail — 3 x 10 each leg  \n5) Standing banded hip abduction/extension — 3 x 12–15  \n6) Core/mobility: bird‑dog 3 x 10; pelvic floor breathing 3 sets\n\nSession B — Upper (conservative)\n1) Warmup  \n2) Machine chest press incline or seated DB press (moderate) — 3 x 8–10  \n3) Chest‑supported row or single‑arm DB row — 3 x 8–10  \n4) Seated lateral raise / banded overhead work — 3 x 12  \n5) Pull‑downs or assisted pull‑up — 3 x 8–10  \n6) Scapular stability + band face pulls — 3 x 15  \n7) Core: Pallof press 3 x 10 each side; diaphragmatic breathing/pelvic floor sets\n\nSession C — Functional & prep for postpartum\n1) Warmup  \n2) Trap‑bar or kettlebell deadlift — 3 x 6–8 (moderate)  \n3) TRX single‑leg squat or supported RFE split squat — 3 x 8–10 each leg  \n4) Cable anti‑rotation chops (controlled) — 3 x 8–10 each side (avoid large trunk rotation)  \n5) Loaded carry variations — 3 x 30–60s  \n6) Core & pelvic floor: standing pelvic floor squeezes + Pallof holds; gentle quadruped rocking for diaphragm\n\nSubstitutions and equipment choices\n- Replace flat bench press with seated, incline (>45°) or machine chest press.  \n- If balance is an issue, use TRX, rack or bench for support during single‑leg work.  \n- If fatigue or pelvic pressure increases, reduce set count (2 sets) and reps, or substitute to machine versions.  \n- Avoid traditional barbell hip thrust (supine). Use quadruped glute work, standing cable hip extension or banded movements.\n\nNutrition & recovery guidance (concise)\n- Calories: Generally increase daily intake: ~+340 kcal/day in 2nd trimester, ~+450 kcal/day in 3rd trimester. Tailor to baseline body composition and activity; if she’s already lean or training hard, keep a slightly larger surplus to support training and fetal needs. Consult her obstetrician/dietitian for personalized plan.  \n- Protein: Aim ~1.2–1.6 g/kg bodyweight per day (higher end if training hard). Spread evenly across meals.  \n- Micronutrients: Continue prenatal vitamin (folate/folic acid, iron as advised), ensure adequate iron, vitamin D and calcium intake. Avoid high‑dose supplements without clearance.  \n- Hydration & sodium: Drink regularly before/during/after sessions; increase fluid intake in heat. Include electrolytes if prolonged sweating or nausea occurs.  \n- Sleep & recovery: Aim for 7–9 hours sleep; add naps if needed. Use massage, foam rolling, gentle stretching to manage aches.  \n- Weight management: Expect steady weight gain;